# Virtual environments
.venv/

# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo

# Testing
.pytest_cache/
.pytest-tmp/

# Build artifacts
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/
*.egg

# Linting / type checking
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.pyre_configuration

# Frontend tooling (oxlint, vnu-jar)
node_modules/

# Scratch space (experiments, probes, throwaway files)
.scratch/

# Coverage documents the smoke harness and the serve harness write into a
# project's coverage directory (runtime artifact, not source).  The SQLite
# database this pattern used to ignore is no longer produced: rebrew writes
# db/coverage-<target>.toml and recoverage only reads them.
db/
coverage.db
coverage.db-shm
coverage.db-wal
coverage.db.lock
.bun-cache/
.bun-tmp/
web/dist/
.uv-cache/

# The sbom job's three artifacts, by the names it uploads them under. The job
# writes them on the runner, where nothing reads the tree afterwards, but
# `make python-sbom > recoverage-python-sbom.txt` is the documented way to
# reproduce one on a workstation, and a root file an ignore list does not name
# is one `git add .` picks up.  The SPDX document is named by the job too
# (`tools/bundled_js_inventory.py --format spdx --output`), and listing only
# the two .txt files left the third one a `git add .` would have swept into a
# commit; `make browser-sbom-spdx` writes it at the same root.
recoverage-python-sbom.txt
recoverage-browser-sbom.txt
recoverage-browser-spdx.json

# The sibling rebrew checkout, as a link inside this tree.  Nothing here reads
# it: the uv sources table in pyproject.toml and the Makefile both resolve
# `../rebrew`, which is OUTSIDE the work tree, so a link here is not what any
# gate or build resolves.  One was committed anyway, pointing at an absolute
# /tmp path that resolves on the machine that made it and nowhere else: every
# other clone got a dangling entry, and `git ls-files | tar` -- which the build
# job uses to stage its reproducibility rebuild -- carried it into that tree
# verbatim, where it stood in for the sibling checkout the same step links in
# from $GITHUB_WORKSPACE/../rebrew.  A worktree or a contributor who links the
# sibling in place still works; that link is a local convenience, not source.
# tests/test_supply_chain.py (TestSiblingCheckoutIsNotATrackedLink) holds it out
# of the index.
/rebrew
